@node "Introduction" "Introduction to FileKiller"
@{b}@{fg shine}________________________________________________________________________@{ub}@{fg text}

@{b}Introduction@{ub}
@{b}________________________________________________________________________@{ub}

@{b}FileKiller@{ub} is my answer to the door `ByteKiller' which is for the FAME and
AmiExpress systems.  (It also takes some insparation from the other
Nuker/Fakers around but ByteKiller was the main influence.)

@{b}FileKiller@{ub} and ByteKiller are what are know as Nukers or Fakers.  They
origionated on Conference based systems (In the case of ByteKiller,
AmiExpress) but I have found them to be just as useful on a system like Zeus
which has dedicated file areas.

A `Nuker' is a tool, where if while you are browsing a file list and see a
file that is either (for example) :
            @{b}a)@{ub} Illegal
            @{b}b)@{ub} Something you wanted personally

Then you can, in the case of @{b}a)@{ub}, delete the file and then send the user a
message telling them what has happened.  In the case of @{b}b)@{ub}, you would start
FileKiller and write a comment onto the bottom of the file which which will
also be put into the "Thank You" message which will be sent to the user if
you opted to.

If you are not deleting the file, there are also the options to:
            @{b}1)@{ub} Make the file Free to download
               (i.e., doesn't affect a downloaders file ratio)
            @{b}2)@{ub} Write a message into a public area containing
               the files name, date, size and description together with
               a short message from you (note: the file description in
               the message will contain your file comment anyway)

Option @{b}2)@{ub} can be very useful if you wish to announce over a Fidonet or local
news echo that you have this file, and that it's available for File Request
or download.

@{b}FileKiller@{ub} is NONE-CRIPPLED @{"Shareware." link "Registration" 0}

There are no backdoors in this door - you may even request the source code if
you want peace of mind.
@endnode

@node "Install" "Installation"
@{b}@{fg shine}________________________________________________________________________@{ub}@{fg text}

@{b}Installation@{ub}
@{b}________________________________________________________________________@{ub}

Installation is very simple, nothing more than altering your scripts and
dragging an icon!

    @{b}1.@{ub}  Drag the @{b}`FileKiller'@{ub} icon from where ever you extracted the
        archive to your Doors: assign.
    @{b}2.@{ub}  Choose where you want to start @{b}FileKiller@{ub}.  I personally have
        mine as:

            @{b}a)@{ub} a hidden option in the Short File list (using
               control code 202 to only allow CoSys/SIG-Ops to see it.
            @{b}b)@{ub} an option on the main file menu (hidden as
               above again)

    @{b}3.@{ub}  Add the lines:

        GetUserData 'ACCESS'
        If RESULT > 159 then do /* Your lowest SIG-Op level */
            CallScript 'Doors:FileKiller/FileKiller.rexx'
        End

        (Obviously, don't forget to put in the OPTION_x, Signal MENULOOP
        or the functions you use in your scripts)

    That's it!

@{b}@{bg shine}Note:@{ub}@{bg back} @{b}FileKiller@{ub} is compressed with @{i}CompressRexx@{ui} by Robert Hofmann, but @{b}is@{ub}
still ARexx! You @{u}must@{uu} call it with CallScript, and not ZDoor
@endnode

